  Q + H_narrow is contained in I-Sigma_1 (every T_E instance is Sigma_1 induction, every T_N instance open
  induction), so it is strictly weaker than PA; yet it covers the practice and overtakes Q + T_Ind.

(B) u7 natural law G1 (seed 11, as in c4_bdtrc.py): motive skeletons and the skeleton theory
  distinct skeletons among the first  100 data: 23
  distinct skeletons among the first  300 data: 37
  distinct skeletons among the first 1000 data: 73
  distinct skeletons among the first 3000 data: 112
  (rand_motive has depth 2 and quantifiers only over atoms: at most 1 + 7 + 3*49 + 2 = 157 skeletons)
  n= 1000  H_skel (119 templates) covers the data: True;  L(H_skel)-L(H_true): c4 code  +42905.6   identity  +42905.6   prior part +43040;  19 formula contexts; asymptotic slope -20.5 bits per doubling
  n= 3000  H_skel (119 templates) covers the data: True;  L(H_skel)-L(H_true): c4 code  +42838.5   identity  +42838.5   prior part +43040;  19 formula contexts; asymptotic slope -20.5 bits per doubling
  distinct skeletons in 200000 motives drawn from G1 (seed 12): 157
  formula contexts used by H_true on 20000 G1 motives (seed 13): 19
  with all of them, H_skel has 164 templates; the asymptotic slope is (164 - 8)/2 - 19*(9-1)/2 = +2.0 bits per doubling of n (positive: H_skel falls further behind)
  Each G1 motive is a Boolean combination of atoms and of single quantifiers over atoms, so it is logically
  equivalent to a Sigma_2 formula; hence Q + H_skel is contained in I-Sigma_2, strictly weaker than PA.
  It covers G1 but loses to Q + T_Ind by its prior and, asymptotically, by the slope above (if positive).
